\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{Graded dimensions of the generalized kernel}
\date{2026-11-26}

\p{For the \vocabk{cyclic grading}{fdip-016I}, the projections onto
the degree spaces along the sum of the others are
##{\pr_\nu=\ell^{-1}\sum_{j=0}^{\ell-1}\zeta^{-j\nu}g^j.}
Let #{V} be finite dimensional, #{d=\dim_k V}, and let
#{A:V\to V} be a #{k}-linear map satisfying #{gA=\zeta A g}. The generalized kernel
#{\ker A^d} is stable under #{g}. For all degrees #{\nu,\mu},
##{\begin{aligned}
\dim_k V_\nu-\dim_k V_\mu
 &=\dim_k(V_\nu\cap\ker A^d)\\
 &\quad-\dim_k(V_\mu\cap\ker A^d).
\end{aligned}}
These are integer differences. At #{\ell=2} this is the
two-degree index of \ref{fdip-013P}.}

\proof{
\p{Use the finite character sum in \ref{fdip-016E} for the cyclic
group of order #{\ell}, acting by powers of #{g}. Its character
at exponent #{\nu} takes the generating step to #{\zeta^\nu},
so the projections are precisely the displayed sums. This remains
valid at #{\ell=1}, with its one projection #{\bbI_V}. Any
#{g}-stable subspace is preserved by these projections.}

\p{The identity #{gA^j=\zeta^j A^j g} makes both the kernel and
image of each power of #{A} stable under #{g}. Write
#{U=\im A^d}. Images of consecutive powers form a decreasing chain.
Each strict decrease lowers the dimension, and equality persists
under #{A}. Hence #{A} maps #{U} onto itself and is bijective there.
Its #{d}th power is therefore injective on #{U}. Rank-nullity and
#{U\cap\ker A^d=0} give #{V=\ker A^d\oplus U}. On #{U},
#{A} sends degree #{\nu} into degree #{\nu+1}. Its inverse sends
degree #{\nu+1} into degree #{\nu}, by the same commutation
identity restricted to #{U}. Thus the degrees of #{U} all have
the same dimension. Projecting the direct sum to each degree and
subtracting cancels those equal contributions.}
}

\p{For an Artinian and Noetherian module, Mathlib's
\leanref{LinearMap.eventually_isCompl_ker_pow_range_pow} gives
complementary kernel and image for sufficiently large powers.
The equal dimensions here use the cyclic degree shift on the image.}
